#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""e1: the driver of calibration experiment E1-M's pilot (docs/design/calibration-experiment-e1-2026-11-04.md).

Developer side (yellow). Python standard library only. The experiment holds
the harness fixed and varies the research model: four fresh test computers
are made, one arm each; the pilot's arms are the objective file's header
`arms` (a subset that includes sa and sb; arm L was dropped for the pilot of
2026-11-19: the gateway's ChatGPT login) (S-a and S-b swe-2-flash through devin, L gpt-6-luna through
chatgpt, I iceman-contributor-free through opencode-go), the same ten
objectives submitted to every arm at the same moment.

  scripts/e1 setup   [--plan] [--label-suffix S] [--expires-hours H]
  scripts/e1 smoke   [--arms sa,sb,i] [--restamp]
  scripts/e1 credits
  scripts/e1 pilot   --claims C1,C2,C3 --credits N --credits-basis TEXT [--objectives FILE]
  scripts/e1 read    [--verdict] [--claims C1,C2,C3]
  scripts/e1 end

Common: --state-dir DIR (default ~/.cache/choir-e1, outside the repo; keys
(0600), computers.json, bundles, the pilot state). The driver never prints a
key. It derives one 0600 key file per computer from the credential JSON that
scripts/qa_test_computer.mjs writes. It acts only on the four test computers
it records in computers.json; it never takes a computer id from an argument.

What it reads and writes on a test computer (every route is one read in the
code, listed in the design's section 14):
  POST /api/evals/texture-prompt            submit an objective under the arm's overlay
  GET  /api/prompt-bar/submissions/{id}     the submission's trajectory id
  GET  /api/runs?trajectory_id=             the trajectory's runs
  GET  /api/runs/{id}/events                a live count of searches (the cost stop)
  POST /api/runs/{id}/cancel                a run past its 60 minutes, or a stop
  GET  /api/runtime/observability           the build
  GET  /api/platform/texture/publications?doc_id=   is a test document published
  choir trace export / files put|get|mkdir  the bundle; the overlay file
  DELETE /api/files/System/model-policy-overlays/<id>.toml   `end`
The one read of anything else is `scripts/status-paper-usage --json` (ssh to
Node B, read only, no more than once in three minutes), for the stops that
watch the newspaper's searches; it acts on no computer.

The validity rules and the verdicts are pure functions of the exported trace
bundles (read_unit, link_builds, verdicts); scripts/selftest-e1 runs them over
the invented bundles in scripts/testdata/e1/.
"""
